      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}6 x y + 3 y^{2}&=9 x^{2} + 324\\\\- 9 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 324&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 9 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 324$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(- 9 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 324)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(- 9 x^{2} + 6 x y + 3 y^{2} - 324)\\,j\\\\&=(6 x + 6 y)\\,i-(- 18 x + 6 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\18 x - 6 y&=0\\\\y&=3x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}3(3x)^{2}+6x(3x)-9x^{2}-324&=0\\\\36x^{2}-324&=0\\\\x^{2}&=9\\\\x&=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(3,9)$ and $(-3,-9)$."
    },
