      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4 x y + y^{2}&=4 x^{2} + 128\\\\- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 128&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 128$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 128)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 128)\\,j\\\\&=(4 x + 2 y)\\,i-(- 8 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\8 x - 4 y&=0\\\\y&=2x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}1(2x)^{2}+4x(2x)-4x^{2}-128&=0\\\\8x^{2}-128&=0\\\\x^{2}&=16\\\\x&=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(4,8)$ and $(-4,-8)$."
    },
